<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A pointer rounded up to an alignment inside the frame keeps its rounding.
    part_test is U-Boot's ALLOC_CACHE_ALIGN_BUFFER shape (clang -O2):

        char raw[ROUND(d->blksz, 512) + 64 - 1];
        buffer = (unsigned char *)(((unsigned long)raw + 63) & ~63UL);

        0x100019  mov rcx,rsp           ; entry rsp - 0x18
        0x100028  lea rbx,[rcx + rax]   ; minus the allocation
        0x10002c  add rbx,0x3f
        0x100030  and rbx,-0x40

    The entry stack pointer was taken to be 256-aligned, so the add and the
    mask folded into the frame offset and kuna printed &Stack0 - n, which is
    the machine's address only when entry rsp is a multiple of 64 (never on
    x86-64, where it is 8 mod 16). realigned is the control: its prologue
    realigns rsp itself (and rsp,-0x40), and its 64-byte local stays a frame
    variable.
-->
